## 【Root Cause Analysis】

The callback does not await the handler promise. A rejection that occurs after the call returns bypasses the surrounding synchronous try/catch, and the EventConsumer callback can finish before domain processing settles. This source finding does not by itself prove that a production Kafka offset was committed incorrectly.

## 【Production-Grade Fix】

```ts
async (event: { name: EventName; payload: any }, raw: any): Promise<void> => {
  const handler = eventHandlers[event.name];
  if (!handler) {
    logging.info(`No handler found for event: ${event.name}`);
    return;
  }

  try {
    await handler(event, raw);
  } catch (error) {
    logging.error(`Handler failed for event: ${event.name}`, error);
    throw error;
  }
}
```

Re-throwing preserves failure visibility for the Kafka wrapper; retry and offset behavior must then be verified against its actual configuration. Add a test whose handler rejects asynchronously and assert that EventConsumer.start observes the rejection.

